break deterministic (measure-zero ties on random data)\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"uuid\": \"469b2796-deb3-5a83-a7a6-1b2f84b86f20\", \"num_tokens\": 2240, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"correct\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"993dd417-9fb2-5d66-9b81-c1e15a113463\", \"num_tokens\": 2272, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"correct\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"0fc8f5f9-1900-55f4-b7d8-acefecbead69\", \"num_tokens\": 6144, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"correct\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"d6d0eb83-de2e-5a6d-ba23-ee72ae216708\", \"num_tokens\": 2048, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"correct\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"1d554a6c-1d9d-54c7-9ed9-6d4b50c95906\", \"num_tokens\": 2112, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"correct\": false, \"speedup\": null}], \"all_correct\": false, \"geomean_speedup\": null, \"decision\": \"discard\", \"decision_reason\": \"0/5 RUNTIME_ERROR AGAIN, identical to c001/c002. Three structurally distinct kernels now fail the same opaque way, RULING OUT: 3D reshape/broadcast, tl.trans, 3-arg tl.dot, tl.argmax, and int64 kernel stores. The fault is in a surface COMMON to all three fused kernels. Strongest remaining suspects (unobservable without a traceback): (1) software-pipelined K-loop with num_stages>=2 miscompiling to an illegal access on Ampere with a strided dot operand; (2) the strided/transposed w B-operand fed to tl.dot; (3) the non-finite -inf sentinel in where/reductions. c004 removes all three: num_stages=1 (pipeliner off), pre-transpose weight ONCE in torch to a contiguous [D,E] buffer (layout-only data movement, not computation) so the B-operand load is contiguous, and replace -inf with a finite -1e30 (< any sigmoid+bias score, behaves identically for max/min but avoids inf-handling quirks). If c004 also fails, c005 falls back to the two-kernel split (C-SPLIT) so a standard well-trodden GEMM pattern can be validated independently of the mega-epilogue.\", \"cumulative_evals\": 3, \"skills_used\": \"none\", \"next_candidate_plan\": \"c004: fused kernel with num_stages=1, contiguous pre-transposed [D,E] weight (torch layout-only), finite -1e30 sentinel; fully-2D argmax-free epilogue, int32 store + torch int64 cast, BLOCK_M=16, BLOCK_K=64, num_warps=4. Fallback c005 = two-kernel GEMM+routing split if c004 still RUNTIME_ERRORs.\"}\n{\"candidate\": \"c004\", \"parent\": \"c003\", \"source_sha256\": \"c6501e0849e8c581519c7c89a020ae9b35b5e6fcce814546955f0b64a3088c4d\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-17T20:20:25Z\", \"hypothesis\": \"The fault common to all three fused kernels is the fused GEMM+mega-epilogue itself (register/resource pressure + strided dot operand + very large unrolled epilogue). The two-kernel split (C-SPLIT) isolates GEMM (Kernel A: canonical tiled bf16 matmul, fp32 accum, contiguous pre-transposed [D,E] weight, logits[T,256] to DRAM, ONE live [BLOCK_M,256] tensor) from routing (Kernel B: one token/program, whole epilogue on 1D [256]/[8] tensors, trivial register footprint). Using the most robust well-trodden Triton paths should finally compile/run on this stack.\", \"config\": {\"arch\": \"two-kernel split\", \"gemm\": {\"BLOCK_M\": 32, \"BLOCK_K\": 64, \"num_warps\": 8, \"num_stages\": 2}, \"route\": {\"grid\": \"(T,)\", \"num_warps\": 4, \"num_stages\": 1}, \"matmul_precision\": \"bf16\", \"sentinel\": \"-1e30 finite\", \"weight_layout\": \"contiguous [D,E] via torch t().contiguous()\", \"idx\": \"int32 store + torch int64 cast\"}, \"validation\": {\"static_gate_passed\": true, \"static_gate_notes\": \"two score arrays; bias selection-only; fp32 epilogue; top-2 sum per group; 4 groups; finite -1e30 sentinel; top-8 experts; bias-free companion via masked sum; +1e-20 then *scaling; int32->int64 output cast; fp32 weight; per-token routing (grid=T); layout-only weight transpose is data movement not computation\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"uuid\": \"469b2796-deb3-5a83-a7a6-1b2f84b86f20\", \"num_tokens\": 2240, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"correct\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"993dd417-9fb2-5d66-9b81-c1e15a113463\", \"num_tokens\": 2272, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"correct\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"0fc8f5f9-1900-55f4-b7d8-acefecbead69\", \"num_tokens\": 6144, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"correct\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"d6d0eb83-de2e-5a6d-ba23-ee72ae216708\", \"num_tokens\": 2048, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"correct\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"1d554a6c-1d9d-54c7-9ed9-6d4b50c95906\", \"num_tokens\": 2112, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"correct\": false, \"speedup\": null}], \"all_correct\": false, \"geomean_speedup\": null, \"decision\": \"discard\", \"decision_reason\": \"0/5 RUNTIME_ERROR AGAIN, even for a textbook two-kernel split whose Kernel A is a canonical lightweight tiled GEMM (single [32,256] fp32 accumulator -> low register pressure) and whose Kernel B is a trivial per-token routine. Four structurally distinct solutions failing IDENTICALLY, including a low-pressure standard GEMM, RULES OUT: fused-epilogue logic, register pressure, 3D ops, tl.trans, 3-arg dot, tl.argmax, int64 stores, strided weight operand (c004 uses contiguous [D,E]), and the -inf sentinel (c004 uses finite -1e30). Since a benchmark built for Triton kernel optimization must support tl.dot and tl.sigmoid/exp, the remaining UNIVERSAL fixable suspect is a launch-resource condition shared by every candidate: a software-pipelined tl.dot with num_stages>=2 over a [BLOCK_K,256] bf16 B-tile allocates large dynamic shared memory (c001/c002 stages=3 ~96KB; c003/c004 stages=2 ~64KB) exceeding the 48KB static default that needs a cudaFuncAttribute opt-in; if that opt-in misbehaves the launch fails at runtime on every workload. c005 tests this directly: two-kernel split with Kernel A at BLOCK_K=32, num_stages=1, num_warps=4 -> B-tile [32,256] bf16 = 16KB, single-buffered, ~18KB total, well under 48KB, no opt-in needed.\", \"cumulative_evals\": 4, \"skills_used\": \"none\", \"next_candidate_plan\": \"c005: two-kernel split, Kernel A BLOCK_M=32/BLOCK_K=32/num_stages=1/num_warps=4 (shared mem ~18KB, no dynamic-smem opt-in), Kernel B unchanged. If c005 PASSES -> the num_stages>=2 dynamic-shared-memory opt-in was the universal blocker; resume perf tuning from there. If c005 still RUNTIME_ERRORs -> resource hypothesis exonerated; next probe tl.dot config (out_dtype / input_precision / fp16 cast) as the universal cause.\"}\n{\"candidate\": \"c005\", \"parent\": \"c004\", \"source_sha256\": \"adcc3ac1feb559934d44c8c707d7dcd6f8d4531593e6caad2041d7cde93bbbcd\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-17T21:36:30Z\", \"hypothesis\": \"The universal blocker is the num_stages>=2 dynamic-shared-memory opt-in on the pipelined tl.dot. A minimal single-buffered GEMM (Kernel A: BLOCK_M=16, BLOCK_K=32, num_stages=1, num_warps=4 -> ~17KB shared, well under the 48KB static cap, no cudaFuncAttribute opt-in) plus the unchanged per-token Kernel B should finally compile/run.\", \"config\": {\"arch\": \"two-kernel split, minimal resources\", \"gemm\": {\"BLOCK_M\": 16, \"BLOCK_K\": 32, \"num_warps\": 4, \"num_stages\": 1}, \"route\": {\"grid\": \"(T,)\", \"num_warps\": 4, \"num_stages\": 1}, \"matmul_precision\": \"bf16\", \"sentinel\": \"-1e30 finite\", \"weight_layout\": \"contiguous [D,E]\", \"idx\": \"int32 store + torch int64 cast\"}, \"validation\": {\"static_gate_passed\": true, \"static_gate_notes\": \"identical numerics to c004; only GEMM tiling/pipelining reduced (BLOCK_M 32->16, BLOCK_K 64->32, stages 2->1, warps 8->4); routing kernel byte-identical to c004\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"uuid\": \"469b2796-deb3-5a83-a7a6-1b2f84b86f20\", \"num_tokens\": 2240, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"correct\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"993dd417-9fb2-5d66-9b81-c1e15a113463\", \"num_tokens\": 2272, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"correct\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"0fc8f5f9-1900-55f4-b7d8-acefecbead69\", \"num_tokens\": 6144, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"correct\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"d6d0eb83-de2e-5a6d-ba23-ee72ae216708\", \"num_tokens\": 2048, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"correct\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"1d554a6c-1d9d-54c7-9ed9-6d4b50c95906\", \"num_tokens\": 2112, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"correct\": false, \"speedup\": null}], \"all_correct\": false, \"geomean_speedup\": null, \"decision\": \"discard\", \"decision_reason\": \"0/5 RUNTIME_ERROR AGAIN, even for a minimal single-buffered (~17KB shared, num_stages=1) GEMM. This EXONERATES the dynamic-shared-memory / num_stages opt-in hypothesis. Five structurally distinct kernels now fail identically. Decisive elimination-by-necessity argument: SOL-ExecBench is a Triton kernel-optimization benchmark whose reference is matmul-heavy, so tl.dot and the absolute-basic ops (tl.load/store/where/max/min/sum/arange/zeros, integer //, .to() casts) MUST work on this stack -- otherwise NO Triton solution could ever pass. By elimination the one NON-basic op present in 100% of c001-c005 and NOT required by other benchmarks' kernels is tl.sigmoid. Every candidate calls tl.sigmoid on the logits; if tl.sigmoid is renamed/moved/unavailable in this Triton version it would fail identically at compile time on every workload with the opaque RUNTIME_ERROR observed. c006 tests this directly: replace tl.sigmoid(x) with the mathematically identical 1/(1+tl.exp(-x)) (tl.exp is a core always-present intrinsic), changing ONLY that one op vs the otherwise-robust c005 two-kernel split. Clean single-variable attribution: if c006 passes, tl.sigmoid was THE universal bug.\", \"cumulative_evals\": 5, \"skills_used\": \"none\", \"next_candidate_plan\": \"c006: c005 two-kernel split with tl.sigmoid(x) replaced by 1/(1+tl.exp(-x)); everything else byte-identical. If c006 passes -> sigmoid was the universal blocker, resume perf tuning (re-fuse, raise BLOCK_M/BLOCK_K/num_stages). If c006 still errors -> exp/sigmoid exonerated; c007 prob